WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:07.420
BRAM BOUT: All right,
good afternoon, everybody.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.950
My name is Bram Bout, a leader
at Google for Education team.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.260
And I'd like to
talk to you today

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.420
a bit more about
what we're doing

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.640
in higher education,
for higher education,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.520
with higher education to
create more Brittanys, and more

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.750
amazing projects like the
one you just saw up there.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.160
We are going to
talk a little bit

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.110
about the cloud for
universities-- what it

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.160
means, what it can do for you.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.120
Then do a deeper
dive into genomics,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.860
one area in
particular where we've

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.260
seen tremendous use and power
of the cloud in higher education

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.290
research.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.200
We'll do a few breakouts to get
your input and your thoughts

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.770
on what we can do better, what
we can do to work with you.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:52.290
And then we'll wrap it
up in about an hour.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.080
So the Cloud for
Higher Ed, we've

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.540
been in the cloud for
a long time as Google.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.380
We were born in the cloud, as we
would like to say, back in 1999

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.420
or so.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.040
And we've been operating
as a cloud company

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.860
from its very
inception ever since.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.381
So in many ways, we've
been investing in the cloud

00:01:09.381 --> 00:01:10.380
from the very beginning.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.330
But it's only
relatively recently

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.880
that we've begun to take
all that engineering and all

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.630
that power out into the
market and give access

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:23.010
to companies and academia
to do something with that.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.880
If you had an opportunity to
see the keynote yesterday,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.100
I think you heard Eric talk
about the opportunities ahead

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:30.600
of it.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.410
You heard Faffa Lee talk about
the power and the potential

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.590
of artificial intelligence that
can be powered by the cloud.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.870
And what really excites me
about that is that to me,

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:43.830
the academic world
plays a tremendous role

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:47.250
in making that vision
happen for all of us.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.490
There's a lot of work to be done
to make the cloud work for all

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.000
of us, to democratize it, and
to really deliver on the promise

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.030
that it inherently has.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.920
So we're very committed.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.650
I hope you walked
away with that message

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:05.040
at the end of these
couple of days.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.340
We already have more than
a billion end users that

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.479
are using the Google Cloud.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.610
You may not know or
see this directly,

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:15.520
but those are companies that
are using the cloud to power

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.340
services for their users.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.720
We've invested about $30 billion
over the past three years

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:27.930
in Capex to build out
the cloud network, which

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.790
is a substantial
multiple actually

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.930
of what our competitors
are doing in this space.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.730
And we have a global network.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.210
About six regions right now,
and adding nine more this year.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.430
That gives you, again, an
idea of how aggressively we

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.280
were expanding and
investing in this space.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.950
And we're investing heavily in
the networking aspect of this,

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:47.450
too.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.100
So when you are operating
in a Google Cloud,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.070
you essentially are
everywhere worldwide

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.590
through Google's
own private network.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.770
As an example, we just laid
another 60-terabyte-per-second

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:02.070
cable between the United States
and Japan to build out that

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.150
network even further.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:07.391
And we will continue to
invest in that going forward.

00:03:07.391 --> 00:03:09.640
And then lastly, specifically
for the higher education

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:14.740
community, we just recently
in 2016 joined Internet2--

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:17.410
obviously a well-known
organization of research

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.660
universities in
the United States.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.700
And we are working
with them on a number

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.310
of what I think are going to
be very interesting projects

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.850
and ideas to bring the
Google Cloud more easily

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.280
and more powerfully to
the higher education

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:32.140
community in the United States.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.570
And we're working on similar
types of partnerships

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:38.900
internationally to make
that happen as well.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.582
So what does this
all mean for you?

00:03:40.582 --> 00:03:42.290
If you are in higher
education, if you're

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.230
a university or a
research institute,

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:46.270
how can you use this
to your advantage?

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.580
Well, we see essentially
three ways that you can use.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.750
The first way is in the
teaching environment,

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.990
just using it to teach the
next generation of students

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.690
where cloud computing really
should become or should

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:02.080
be, arguably, a part of every
curriculum going forward,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.170
because it is going to be
so pervasive in the future

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.030
workforce that
anyone who comes out

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.030
of school in a couple
of years really

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.210
ought to know about the
cloud, about data science

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.710
and what it can do for
you to make projects,

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.470
products, anything better.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.410
The second one is
around administration--

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.150
how you run your university.

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.700
And that can be the more basic
infrastructure type things,

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.660
but it can also be how do you
use the data that you collect,

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:32.230
the data that you have on
the variety of interactions

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.300
you have with your
students, your faculty,

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.960
your administrative staff.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.380
How do you use
the data to create

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:42.320
insights to run your operation
better-- to do new things?

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.050
And lastly, of
course in research,

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.550
we will talk much
more about that.

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:48.640
So for education, it's
really about helping

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.390
students build what's next.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.030
You saw a great
example of Brittany

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:55.794
just now in the video
at the start of this.

00:04:55.794 --> 00:04:57.460
And as I said, this
is something that we

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.040
should do much more often.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.100
We already have since
about the summer

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.520
of last year, a program in
place for education grants

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:08.320
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.040
So this essentially
allows any faculty

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.550
to apply for credits
for their students.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.430
Currently, it's limited or
restricted, if you like,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.900
to faculty of universities
that are providing a computer

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.540
science type of education.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.350
We're kind of looking
at expanding that.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:29.320
And up until now, it has been
restricted to the United States

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:29.890
as well.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:33.940
But I'm very happy to
announce that as of this week,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.690
we're opening that
up to most of Europe,

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:39.860
as well as a few
countries outside of that.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:42.830
And we will continue to
expand that globally.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.730
So the list of eligible
countries is here.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.160
And if you're from
any one of those,

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.380
please take a look at
cloud.google.com/edu and see

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.020
if you can get your
folks, your faculty,

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.970
interested in signing
up for credits.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:05:59.680
Get some exposure,
get some experience

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.560
with what the cloud can do for
you in the computer science

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:04.360
environment, and
obviously beyond that.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.770
An example of how this is being
used to make it kind of come

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:13.360
to life a little bit is in
California State University

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.580
in San Bernardino.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:20.710
One group of students
there is using the cloud

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.450
to really understand networking.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:26.140
So they're using the cloud to
configure networks, firewalls,

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:28.870
different routing
mechanisms, creating

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:32.050
false denial of service
attacks to see how networks

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:33.670
react to this and so forth.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:36.550
And they can do all of that just
sitting behind a computer using

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.610
the cloud as their,
essentially, virtual network

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.520
that they're running
to see and bring

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.970
a lot of what they are learning
in school into practice.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:48.820
Another example-- you see
the pictures off here--

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.070
is for students that are not
actually computer science

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:52.870
students, but
engineering students that

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:56.390
are building a little Rover
with a Raspberry Pi in it,

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:59.020
with some Python scripts
that is talking to the cloud.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.910
And it allows them to
essentially remotely control

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:05.170
that device using a computer
anywhere on campus--

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.210
just a really great
interesting project

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:09.566
to illustrate the
power of the cloud

00:07:09.566 --> 00:07:10.690
and what it can do for you.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:16.270
Then on the
administrative side, this

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:18.520
is all about being
more efficient,

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.370
and being more
effective, quite frankly,

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:25.110
with your infrastructure.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.960
We have a massive
number of products

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:32.100
out there that will
allow you to supplement,

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.940
or if you want to replace
parts of your infrastructure,

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:38.030
in a very efficient manner--

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:41.670
whether that is basic things
like storage or disaster risk

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.250
recovery approaches,
or whether that's

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.070
a more advanced
use of computing,

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.710
like for instance Burse capacity
for your high performance

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.876
computing network.

00:07:51.876 --> 00:07:53.250
All of those things
are possible.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.860
And I think what we
are very proud of is

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:01.454
we have a very cost-effective
and efficient way to do that.

00:08:01.454 --> 00:08:02.870
We also want to
make sure that you

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:04.910
think about what the
cloud can do for you

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:06.470
to make operations run better.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:08.480
How can you go to
the next frontier?

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.160
How can you use the data
you have to do new things?

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:12.770
And one of the topics
that has come up

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:15.410
a lot in our conversations
with universities

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:17.420
is, for instance, the
topic of student success.

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:21.510
Like, how can you improve the
success rate of your students?

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.624
How can you intervene
earlier in the process

00:08:23.624 --> 00:08:25.040
if you feel that
they're beginning

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.310
to go off the rails a
little bit and catch them

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:29.825
before they truly fall off?

00:08:29.825 --> 00:08:31.860
So there's a lot of
opportunities to do that,

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.130
and we have a number of
products to work with that.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:36.799
On the efficiency side, we
have very flexible billing

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:37.309
arrangement.

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:39.350
We actually acquired a
company called Orbitera,

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:42.860
if you are familiar with
that, a couple of months ago.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:46.160
They offer, essentially,
a billing overlay

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:47.780
across multiple cloud
providers so you

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:50.970
can have it all in one
place and really see

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.080
who is spending what where
without necessarily having

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:57.050
to get in the way as a
central IT department

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.152
and sort of become the
bottleneck for everything

00:08:59.152 --> 00:09:00.860
that's happening in
the university, which

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:05.330
is from what we understand
pretty much impossible.

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:07.620
We also offer a lot
more flexibility.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:09.470
We have, for instance,
customizable VM's.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.700
You don't have to choose
two, four, or eight cores.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.120
You can choose five, if
that's what you need.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:16.400
Any type of memory
configuration, bandwidth,

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.640
and so forth-- it's all very
flexible, billed by minutes,

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:22.340
so you don't pay
in large chunks.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.790
And we have automatic
sustainable use discounts,

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:28.280
which means that you don't
have to reserve an entity

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.690
to get good pricing.

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.520
If you use an entity
a lot, you simply

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:33.110
get a better price,
because that's

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:36.174
what a sustained use
discount just for you--

00:09:36.174 --> 00:09:37.340
as well as future discounts.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:39.820
If we change our pricing,
and I'm sure we will,

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:42.750
you actually benefit from
that immediately, regardless

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:46.520
of the agreements
you have with us.

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.260
On the Big Data
side, we have a lot

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:49.910
of tools that are
very powerful here--

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:55.100
BigQuery, TensorFlow for
machine learning and kind of new

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:59.220
algorithms, particularly good
for structured and unstructured

00:09:59.220 --> 00:09:59.720
data.

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:01.970
So combinations of
things that are typically

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:04.190
difficult to manage in
a more classic database

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.110
environment, that's
an area where

00:10:06.110 --> 00:10:11.720
I think our models and
our products excel.

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:13.790
Hopefully you've had a
chance to learn a bit more

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:15.740
about that in any
of the sessions

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.420
here over the last
couple of days.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:18.795
And if you have
any questions, we

00:10:18.795 --> 00:10:22.590
can talk much more about
that a little bit later.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:25.310
Lastly, and perhaps
most excitedly,

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:28.640
we want to give you the
power to change the future.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.070
We met with a large research
university a couple of weeks

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:33.980
ago and we were talking
about the power of the cloud,

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:36.362
and what can it do, and
how can we work with you.

00:10:36.362 --> 00:10:37.820
And they were
saying, yeah, we love

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:40.940
this, because really we
want a partnership where

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:42.290
we can change the world.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:46.640
We think there is things we
can do in the medical fields

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.440
or in the field
even of humanities

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:51.410
where we can have a dramatic
impact if we work together

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:56.020
and bring the power of the
cloud to our research community

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.970
to really do amazing things.

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:01.970
Again, we have the basics
in terms of affordability

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.970
and access, but we have
also very innovative tools

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:06.950
around machine learning
and advanced APIs.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:10.730
And I think we are very
confident and proud that we

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.880
have these kind of
bleeding-edge innovations,

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:15.770
and that we bring
them into production

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:18.620
and make them
available to everyone

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:20.750
as quickly as possible.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:22.940
So recent innovations
like TensorFlow,

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:24.920
but also Cloud
Spanner, for instance,

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:29.630
as a globally
consistent SQL database,

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:32.360
are all things
that make it easier

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.640
for researchers to use
the power of these tools

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.250
very efficiently.

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:39.290
And lastly, you can
start where you are.

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:41.474
We don't advocate
the replacement

00:11:41.474 --> 00:11:43.640
of what you have, and just
kind of go into the cloud

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:45.015
and leave everything
else behind.

00:11:45.015 --> 00:11:47.210
We really want to
work with you to see

00:11:47.210 --> 00:11:51.200
where we can begin to work with
you to expand what you can do.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.580
Again, overflow for your high
performance computing center.

00:11:55.580 --> 00:12:00.440
Or even just like a weekend-type
burst for something very

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.130
specific-- something we did
with CERN a while back--

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:06.590
can be extremely powerful,
and actually surprisingly

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:09.010
affordable.

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:10.094
We have a ton of products.

00:12:10.094 --> 00:12:12.259
I'm obviously not going to
go through each of those.

00:12:12.259 --> 00:12:13.910
But to just give
you a feel for that,

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:15.460
and if you walk
around here and have

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:17.260
been able to attend
some of these sessions,

00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:19.510
I'm sure you've learned
about some of these.

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.640
We have a ton of products
in data and analytics.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:23.770
We have products in
application development,

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:25.735
all the way from
completely managed--

00:12:25.735 --> 00:12:27.730
you just give us
the source code--

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:30.400
all the way back to you do it
all yourself, and pretty much

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.350
everything in between.

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:34.930
We have lots of tools and
products around infrastructure

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:37.900
and operations to make your life
easier, whether that's logging,

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:43.090
or log analysis, or
billing and accounting.

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.344
So there's a lot of places
where you can start.

00:12:45.344 --> 00:12:47.260
And I think one of the
challenges in some ways

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.060
is to figure out
exactly how we're

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:52.240
going to go about that, how
we're going to work with you.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:55.840
Now for many of you, a lot of
these tools may appear new.

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.150
And to some extent, they
are, in that we've only

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:01.540
made them available externally
relatively recently.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.060
But take something
like MapReduce,

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:06.490
for instance, which was
essentially developed by Google

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:10.409
engineers in 2004,
a very long time ago

00:13:10.409 --> 00:13:12.700
in the form of white papers,
and has been in production

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:18.280
within Google since 2004, that
sort of spun off into Hadoop--

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.550
like an open-source platform
for that particular thing.

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:23.650
The same thing with Bigtable.

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:26.740
BigQuery is internally
used in Google

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:30.100
as called Dremel, and has
been there since 2008,

00:13:30.100 --> 00:13:32.020
and is still heavily used today.

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:35.920
Even the Cloud Spanner that was
just announced very recently

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:38.440
has been in production
in Google since 2012.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:42.310
So the benefits you have here
is that you have products that

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:45.130
are cutting-edge, really
state of the art kind of ways

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:47.410
to think about some of
the big computer science

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:51.670
problems of our time, but
that have been tested,

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:54.310
improved, and hardened
at massive scale

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:58.420
within Google-- because
we do a few bytes of data.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.900
And you get the benefit of that
in your production environment.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:05.200
The moment you switch it
on, you have the knowledge

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:07.120
that this has been
tried and tested

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.280
in a very harsh
environment already

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:11.200
for a substantial
amount of time.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:13.060
So that's one of
the main things I

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:15.370
think sets us apart from
some of our competitors,

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:17.980
is that we have tremendous
innovation, particularly

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.140
in the field of big
data machine learning--

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:22.630
and that innovation has
been tried and tested

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:27.710
and will continue to
accelerate over time.

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:30.730
One example of
that is with Cern.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:34.180
This is one of their
collider units, I think.

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:36.415
I'm not sure exactly
what this is,

00:14:36.415 --> 00:14:39.800
but it looks very impressive.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:40.340
It's what?

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:41.500
Sorry?

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:42.640
The particle accelerator.

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:43.430
There you go.

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:46.760
So what we did with
them-- obviously,

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.970
these generate massive
amounts of data

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.670
that need to be processed,
need to be visualized,

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:54.370
need to be dealt
with in some way.

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:58.030
So we've become a partner
of their high energy cloud--

00:14:58.030 --> 00:15:00.220
high energy physics cloud.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:03.940
And we're now helping them
and the researchers that

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:07.510
want to use this data to process
this and to churn through this.

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:10.180
And we've done experiments
and projects with them

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:13.270
where we've literally spun up
100,000 cores over a period

00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:16.780
of, let's say, a weekend, to
do a very rapid burst of data

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:18.640
analysis that would
have taken months

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:21.010
in any other environment,
quite frankly,

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:22.570
to deliver these
kinds of results

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:25.259
that we were able to do in
a very short period of time.

00:15:25.259 --> 00:15:26.800
And that's the really
exciting stuff.

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:30.222
And there's a lot more that
we can do together with them.

00:15:30.222 --> 00:15:32.180
The last example I'll
give you of a partnership

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:34.490
is the one with the
National Science Foundation.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:37.070
We just announced this
a couple of weeks back,

00:15:37.070 --> 00:15:41.030
a partnership for their Big
Data Sciences and Engineering

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:44.240
project, where they issued
a call for proposals,

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:44.830
essentially.

00:15:44.830 --> 00:15:46.890
And Google is one
of the providers.

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:50.150
We're committing $3 billion
in credits for grants

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:54.350
that the NSF is going to give
for research in this field,

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:56.967
where you can use the Google
Cloud and all of its power,

00:15:56.967 --> 00:15:58.550
and all of the
products that we talked

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:02.180
about to continue to develop
Big Data and the science

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:03.320
behind this.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:04.781
So we're very
excited about this.

00:16:04.781 --> 00:16:07.280
We're very excited about working
with a higher ed community.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:09.320
We've obviously been
partnering with many of you

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:13.700
for a very long time already,
largely in the area of G Suite.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.310
And we're very excited about
opening this next chapter

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:17.480
around the cloud.

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:20.010
We see tremendous
opportunities for innovation,

00:16:20.010 --> 00:16:22.520
whether it's in teaching,
whether it's administration,

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:24.200
whether it's research,
there's a lot

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:25.580
of things we can do together.

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:26.480
We're very committed.

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:29.900
I hope you walk away with
that from not just this talk,

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:31.900
but from this entire conference.

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:35.690
And we really see some fantastic
opportunities to work with you.

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:38.540
So with that, I'll
hand it over to Benny

00:16:38.540 --> 00:16:40.800
who's going to talk
more about genomics.

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:41.300
Thank you.

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:49.765
BENNY AYALEW: Thank you
all for having me today.

00:16:49.765 --> 00:16:52.390
I just want to punch
through a number of things

00:16:52.390 --> 00:16:57.010
here in the next
20 or so minutes--

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:59.740
everything ranging from
security and compliance

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:03.850
to the reality of dealing
with both research

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:06.339
and academic
environment, as well as

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:09.130
clinical environments
as it pertains

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.230
to the world of genomics.

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:13.599
And then if the demo
gods are with me,

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:16.000
I'm going to go ahead and
walk through a petabyte

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.150
worth of cancer genomic
data sort of live,

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:22.030
and tempt the network here.

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:23.500
So we'll have a bit of fun.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:28.980
So the very first thing I
wanted to sort of touch on

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:34.740
is the typical question
that we get out from folks

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:37.110
that we deal with
in academia and also

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.240
in clinical environments.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:41.290
This is a bit of an
elephant in the room,

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:44.940
so we hear this all the time.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:49.980
You've probably attended
your fair share of sessions

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.780
around security and compliance
in all of the other breakouts

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.780
that we've had, so I won't
belabor those points.

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:57.690
But it's really
important to understand

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:03.060
when we get into this space
of data ownership and data

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:07.280
locality, any time you start to
deal with very sensitive data

00:18:07.280 --> 00:18:10.830
sets-- phenotypic data sets,
things that are actually

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:15.630
subject to HIPAA, folks get very
understandably very concerned

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:19.500
around what cloud providers
actually have to offer,

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:23.550
and what sort of
capabilities and where

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:27.330
the lines of demarcations are
for whose responsibility it

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:30.570
is to store and
safeguard that data.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:33.420
So we'll run through most
of the answers for these.

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:38.280
But the main thing to
remember is that, number one,

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:39.340
we do sign BAA's.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:45.980
And we do, in fact, have some
examples for you on that.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:47.860
But very often when
you start to look

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:52.870
at the regulatory and compliance
templates to which Google

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:59.530
itself holds itself accountable
through external audits,

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:05.560
it very often meets and
frequently exceeds the letter

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:07.900
and the spirit of the
controls stipulated

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:11.350
in a lot of these
data protections.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.350
So the very first
example I'll talk through

00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:18.200
is the Clinical Genomics
Service at Stanford University.

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:20.640
I've had the
tremendous privilege

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:22.390
of being able to work
with the folks there

00:19:22.390 --> 00:19:25.720
over the last few years.

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:29.800
And this was sort of a
collaboration that started out

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:32.830
in very early days
for Google in terms

00:19:32.830 --> 00:19:36.010
of sort of foraying
into that space,

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:40.420
and really resulted in this
ongoing and very virtuous

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:44.320
collaboration between both
the clinical environments,

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:50.110
as well as the School of
Medicine within Stanford.

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:56.140
So just a quick recap around the
use cases in Dr. Merker's lab

00:19:56.140 --> 00:19:59.200
and in that
environment, the pilot

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:03.940
kicked off sort of in the
January 2014 time frame.

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:07.360
And this is an area
where, obviously, Stanford

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:10.600
being world-renowned, is
an area that actually folks

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:14.260
turn to when other care
providers have been completely

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:17.620
stumped in terms of
investigating very

00:20:17.620 --> 00:20:22.690
rare diseases, familial
diseases, things that have been

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:26.760
very, very difficult to diagnose
and get to the root cause of.

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:29.320
And obviously, genomics
has been shedding light

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:32.350
into how some of
those can actually

00:20:32.350 --> 00:20:37.972
be a little better diagnosed
and a little better treated.

00:20:37.972 --> 00:20:40.180
So some of the challenges
that they actually ran into

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:43.389
was not knowing what
sort of ends they were

00:20:43.389 --> 00:20:44.680
dealing with right off the bat.

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:46.970
I mean, people can estimate
to see how many cases

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:49.450
you might get in
a particular area,

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:52.120
but imagine having to
build capacity and think

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:57.100
about putting out the
capital expenditure

00:20:57.100 --> 00:21:01.970
with an uncertain amount
of investment in mind.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:04.510
The other aspect also
is that there's just

00:21:04.510 --> 00:21:07.660
a tremendous amount of
dynamism between the folks

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:11.830
who are in clinical environments
on the hospital side

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:14.649
providing care, but also
very actively collaborate

00:21:14.649 --> 00:21:15.940
on the School of Medicine side.

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:19.240
So a person who might be
a given Google identity

00:21:19.240 --> 00:21:23.180
might navigate across regulated
and controlled boundaries,

00:21:23.180 --> 00:21:26.129
and so how do you sort
of solve for that?

00:21:26.129 --> 00:21:27.670
And those were some
of the challenges

00:21:27.670 --> 00:21:30.140
that we overcame together.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:34.540
The bit around elasticity
was a significant problem

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:35.950
to overcome.

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:38.620
And of course,
obviously, for that,

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:41.140
the Google Compute Engine
and the Cloud Platform

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:45.620
were able to be a fit there.

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:49.470
The other aspect also was
flexibility in the language

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:50.470
in our terms of service.

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:54.330
Of course, obviously,
the lawyer cats,

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:57.640
as we like to call
them in Googleland,

00:21:57.640 --> 00:21:59.590
have a good bit
of back and forth

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:02.890
in terms of the red
lines that typify

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:05.140
the sensitivities around this.

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:08.050
But we were just in a
fantastic partnership together

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:11.620
and were able to forge
this alliance going forward

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:17.320
in tackling both the
technical and the legal issues

00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:21.370
that were required for making
this service actually light up.

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:26.580
And so for that, there
were a number of solutions

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:28.800
that actually came into being.

00:22:28.800 --> 00:22:32.580
Obviously, Google Cloud Platform
solved the storage aspect

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:33.840
of issues.

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:37.930
Google Genomics is
a managed service

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:40.900
that actually allows
for a genomic sequence

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.240
analysis on our
platform where you

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:46.630
don't have to spin up
machines and download

00:22:46.630 --> 00:22:48.160
your favorite
bioinformatics tool,

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:50.493
and then compile it, and then
run it, and go through all

00:22:50.493 --> 00:22:54.370
of that common back and forth.

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:57.650
You just basically bring your
data and then invoke the API,

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:01.930
and get your reads and variants
in that particular area.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:04.990
BigQuery obviously was
also a huge part of this,

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:06.760
because in the
world of genomics,

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:10.470
which is a very sort of
staged analysis world--

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:12.280
the tertiary
analysis, or the sort

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:16.930
of so-called so what
phase, is an area

00:23:16.930 --> 00:23:19.480
where we've been hosting
a tremendous amount

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:22.390
of public data sets that
we've made available

00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:24.270
that I'll walk you through.

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:28.810
And so this is basically a
conglomeration of both managed

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:32.050
services, as well
as unique capability

00:23:32.050 --> 00:23:35.210
that exists on our platform
that we're able to provide

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:37.750
the solution for Stanford.

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:40.330
And I reckon that
speaks for itself.

00:23:40.330 --> 00:23:45.640
From my good friend
Somalee Datta

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.930
in terms of tackling
these problems,

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:49.510
these are the typical
issues that we

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:51.070
tend to see in
campus environments

00:23:51.070 --> 00:23:54.760
where, yes, the campus
cluster was sufficient

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:57.610
for a number of analyzes,
and will continue

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:02.920
to be viable and useful for
the time period going forward.

00:24:02.920 --> 00:24:07.210
But when it starts to get to
burst analysis and being able

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:10.000
to deal with
multiples of hundreds

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:14.830
of terabytes' and often into
the petabytes' worth of space,

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:16.840
that's really the
space for cloud,

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:19.720
and it's really an end
world where the data sets

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:22.000
and the analysis pipelines
are straddled between both

00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.640
environments, and it's not an
or solution where everything

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:26.590
shifts over from
one to the other.

00:24:29.150 --> 00:24:31.490
Another example of
what we were able to do

00:24:31.490 --> 00:24:35.480
around genomic research
was our collaboration

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:38.270
with Autism Speaks.

00:24:38.270 --> 00:24:42.340
And here's an area where
it's actually multinational.

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:51.170
There are cohorts of autistic
children's and familial sort

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:54.410
of quintuplets
quints of data sets

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:57.080
that actually are contained
in this environment that

00:24:57.080 --> 00:25:03.020
are used by researchers both
inside the US and, of course,

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:04.131
abroad as well.

00:25:04.131 --> 00:25:05.630
And so what we were
able to do there

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:08.750
was almost analogous
to the Stanford case

00:25:08.750 --> 00:25:11.360
where we provided both
solutions, as well

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:15.350
as the open access to that
dataset for researchers

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:17.580
to be able to come in
and make use of it.

00:25:20.180 --> 00:25:22.450
Another area that I'll just
jump into in the spirit

00:25:22.450 --> 00:25:25.280
of the opening
video to the session

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:30.040
is really around the
area of cancer research.

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:34.060
The Institute for Systems
Biology have, in my mind,

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:38.800
done a very exemplary job of
managing that really fine line

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:43.780
between allowing an environment
for researchers and clinicians

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:47.290
to come in with very
sensitive and private data,

00:25:47.290 --> 00:25:49.360
but then be able
to do that analysis

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:52.570
in the context of public data
sets like the Cancer Genome

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:55.450
Atlas, which, by the
way, in its natural form

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:59.530
sits on a government site
nicely zipped up and weighs in

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:01.020
at a petabyte.

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:04.870
And so if you're a
cancer researcher

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:07.360
and you want to do that unit
of work, which is a tumor

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:10.886
normal peer analysis, your
step zero of that pipeline

00:26:10.886 --> 00:26:12.385
is go ahead and
download a petabyte.

00:26:12.385 --> 00:26:15.880
That make get you very unpopular
with some of your storage

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:17.800
admins on campus.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:20.877
But we've actually made
that a very simple exercise

00:26:20.877 --> 00:26:21.460
to go through.

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:24.640
And so I'm going to
go ahead and jump

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:27.910
through a couple of live
demos just to give you

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:30.370
a sense of what that
actually looks like

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:32.450
and what that feels like.

00:26:32.450 --> 00:26:39.130
So this is the ISB site, and
it's actually open in public.

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:43.300
And I think the part I was
saying that was very exemplary

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:48.490
is really depicted in this
part of the web page done

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:51.750
by the team over at ISB.

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:54.760
And this really solves the
three key constituents,

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.950
which is the principal
investigator,

00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:58.450
the computational
researcher who's

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:01.300
very, very comfortable
in Python, or R, or just

00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:05.500
in SQL, and then of course,
the algo developer who

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:10.270
doesn't want to see anything
but usually a command prompt.

00:27:10.270 --> 00:27:12.040
The red boxes
there are obviously

00:27:12.040 --> 00:27:14.650
signifying user data,
which is completely private

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:17.020
to the individual
researcher, not

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:21.730
for group sharing
or public sharing.

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:27.610
And then the availability
of open access data,

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:29.170
which is obviously
the Cancer Genome

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:32.620
Atlas, and a number of
other data sets of interest

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:36.669
and of high value for
analysis are signified there

00:27:36.669 --> 00:27:37.210
in the green.

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:40.810
And then the orange
one is a peculiar one,

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:46.660
and that actually
goes into the database

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:48.250
of phenotypes and genotypes.

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:49.390
It's called DB Cap.

00:27:49.390 --> 00:27:52.360
And it's essentially a
controlled access dataset

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:57.400
that has sometimes identifiable
data intended for consumption

00:27:57.400 --> 00:27:59.950
by qualified researchers

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:02.800
So you can now
imagine a world where

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:05.170
the storage is taken care of.

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:07.480
And for qualified
researchers, they

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:09.730
can now have sort
of a workbench area

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:12.340
where they can
actually do their work

00:28:12.340 --> 00:28:14.800
and do their analysis
in the context of both

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:17.560
their private data sets
that might actually

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:20.290
belong to a patient
that they're treating

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:23.860
or a clinical trial that they
may be a part of, but also

00:28:23.860 --> 00:28:27.280
in context of larger public data
sets, and as well as controlled

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:30.360
access datasets, without having
to go and change their work

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:32.800
bench or retool in any way.

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:35.530
So they can do that for storage,
they can do that with BigQuery

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:37.780
for tertiary analysis,
and then they

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:39.880
can do it using our
Google Genomics.

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:41.560
And they're able to
pull off this magic

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:44.680
without having to give up
any level of flexibility

00:28:44.680 --> 00:28:46.910
to the lower left
that you see there,

00:28:46.910 --> 00:28:50.410
which is that world of
complete open pipelines

00:28:50.410 --> 00:28:53.230
where you can now bring in
your favorite Docker container

00:28:53.230 --> 00:28:58.180
and install this newest
version of an analysis tool

00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:00.550
and still be able to take
part in this virtual cycle

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:04.760
without having to
really break anything.

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:08.620
So as we jump into that,
you can see the first thing

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:12.790
that happens is it'll prompt
you for your OAuth credentials.

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:17.690
And it just opens up
with this blank space.

00:29:17.690 --> 00:29:23.500
And it has the notion
of a workbook concept.

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:29.620
And that workbook can use all
sorts of different analysis

00:29:29.620 --> 00:29:34.900
types and different plot types
that you can now put into it.

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:39.080
But just as interestingly,
the business

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:41.870
of creating cohorts from
very large data sets

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:45.120
is a unit of work
in cancer research.

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:47.420
So don't blink for this
part, because we're

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:53.180
going to click this bit,
and what you'll see is--

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:54.870
OK, that just
happened way too fast.

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:57.710
I didn't even get
a spinner on it.

00:29:57.710 --> 00:30:01.740
That just loaded and
visualized the entire TCGA

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:05.220
all on a single page,
which is extraordinary,

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:07.520
because this sort of passes
the [INAUDIBLE] test.

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:13.290
If you can get a CS guy to walk
through cancer genomic data

00:30:13.290 --> 00:30:16.660
and make some sense of
it, and navigate it,

00:30:16.660 --> 00:30:20.880
then I think it's met
the usability test.

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:22.560
So if I now want to
go ahead and create

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:30.330
a cohort of, say, all females--

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:32.220
and I'm interactively
walking through this

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:34.970
and it's just spinning it
up as it's going along--

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:40.890
whose cancer was diagnosed
between those age ranges

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:48.780
and who are smokers, as
just a simple example.

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:54.300
And now I've whittled this down
to 115 smokers who are female

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:56.080
and between the
ages of 10 and 39.

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:58.950
Now I can go ahead and
walk through and figure out

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:01.785
if these cohorts were
part of any given study--

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:07.200
which one of them
are alive or dead,

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:12.370
sample types, where the
tissue was actually picked up.

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:15.250
And I can now go in and
save this as a cohort,

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:18.190
and then share it with
another collaborator.

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:19.870
One of the other
things also is that you

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:25.210
have some additional data,
like gene expression and mRNA,

00:31:25.210 --> 00:31:29.080
and all sorts of
things that are clearly

00:31:29.080 --> 00:31:33.730
of interest to people who
would work with this data

00:31:33.730 --> 00:31:36.320
over and over.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:39.130
So now I'll go ahead and choose
to save this as a cohort.

00:31:39.130 --> 00:31:41.980
And I'll just say--

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:43.870
OK, e-mails.

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:53.230
Maybe if I switched off the
search page, that might work.

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:54.382
Hang on here.

00:31:57.990 --> 00:32:02.110
I'll just type the
word test in there.

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:06.220
So once this cohort has now
been created, what I can do

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:09.610
is go ahead and share it
with another collaborator.

00:32:09.610 --> 00:32:13.060
And say, hey, for that clinical
trial that you're working with,

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:18.070
how does this particular cohort
look like in your analysis?

00:32:18.070 --> 00:32:20.220
The other aspect also
is if I've got multiples

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:21.670
of these I can now
start to do set

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:24.370
operations on these--
not in, or complement,

00:32:24.370 --> 00:32:26.339
or intersection, and so on.

00:32:26.339 --> 00:32:27.880
So these are units
of work that would

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:31.540
have required lots and lots
of coding, and lots and lots

00:32:31.540 --> 00:32:35.590
of data wrangling, which
are now made very, very

00:32:35.590 --> 00:32:38.140
interactive and useful.

00:32:38.140 --> 00:32:40.630
And then the part that
I was mentioning around

00:32:40.630 --> 00:32:47.840
that DbGap credential is that
for the same cohort that I just

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:50.720
now created, if I am
a qualified researcher

00:32:50.720 --> 00:32:55.880
in an academic environment, I
can now go in and put my DbGap

00:32:55.880 --> 00:33:00.080
details into this
page and authenticate

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:02.990
into that secure environment.

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:05.780
And now I can get additional
data brought back,

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:08.660
additional results brought
back, from my same query.

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:10.880
I don't need to go and
recreate it all over again.

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:13.790
It's a very, very
seamless integration.

00:33:13.790 --> 00:33:17.780
So those are just a
couple of examples

00:33:17.780 --> 00:33:20.600
I wanted to walk you through.

00:33:20.600 --> 00:33:24.860
We've gone ahead and
created on Google Genomics

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:37.230
a tremendous amount
of content intended

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:43.610
for consumption by researchers
and folks in academia.

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:44.610
There's our GitHub page.

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:47.400
I meant to actually
go to this page.

00:33:47.400 --> 00:33:51.860
The very first item
that you'll see--

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:54.680
the keywords are Google
Genomics cookbook--

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:58.650
is this very rich and
continuingly growing

00:33:58.650 --> 00:34:02.000
data set that,
again, really removes

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:05.630
the friction out of having to
do a bunch of heavy lifting

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:12.980
and copying and duplicating and
localizing of data to just get

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:16.159
the work done of
continuing the research,

00:34:16.159 --> 00:34:18.380
rather than dealing
with the data wrangling.

00:34:18.380 --> 00:34:21.020
So those are just a
couple of examples

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:25.080
I wanted to go ahead
and walk you through.

00:34:25.080 --> 00:34:31.909
And obviously, within research
and collaborative environments,

00:34:31.909 --> 00:34:35.679
notebooks are a very popular
item and environment.

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:38.540
So one thing I wanted to just
sort of quickly leave you with

00:34:38.540 --> 00:34:42.618
is that we also have Google
Cloud's data lab environment,

00:34:42.618 --> 00:34:43.909
which is really hosted Jupyter.

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:48.139
And it allows folks to be
able to connect naturally

00:34:48.139 --> 00:34:50.000
into BigQuery query
and everything else

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:54.300
in our environments,
utilizing this environment.

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:56.600
So that's my piece.

00:34:56.600 --> 00:34:58.380
Thank you.

